      ],
      "operationalSteps": [
        {
          "step": 1,
          "learnerAction": "Check stock labels/concentrations, hazard instructions, cuvette path length and instrument channel; label all tubes before pipetting. Record actual temperature."
        },
        {
          "step": 2,
          "learnerAction": "Prepare the named blanks and C1–C4 with separate clean tips. Mix and cap. Warm up the instrument according to its manual; record blank readings separately for high-Fe standards and low-Fe sample matrix."
        },
        {
          "step": 3,
          "learnerAction": "Measure C1–C4 at480nm at25±0.5°C. Subtract the high-Fe blank. Check monotonicity/linearity and the supplied reference band shape. Re-read a middle standard and a blank after the sample; stop if drift exceeds0.005absorbance or residuals exceed0.010. Record readings, not just a drawn straight line."
        },
        {
          "step": 4,
          "learnerAction": "Prepare S and independent repeat from the exact volumes. Mix gently, close and start the timer. Measure S in its capped cuvette without chemical reagents, dilution, heating, extraction or drying. Re-read the same S at60,90,120seconds after mixing and record temperature. Wait longer if values drift by more than0.005; retain the sequence and its times."
        },
        {
          "step": 5,
          "learnerAction": "Record the original product band and the low-Fe/SCN-only blank comparison before any perturbation. Product evidence, initial totals and inferred residual reactants must refer to this one original composition/time plateau. Compare the independently prepared repeat without pretending it is the same physical sample."
        },
        {
          "step": 6,
          "learnerAction": "After the original-state readings are complete, use a separate labelled aliquot for the fresh dilution test. Document its new total volume, time and return to a stable signal; never back-transfer its changed composition to the original S."
        },
        {
          "step": 7,
          "learnerAction": "Use the corrected product signal and calibrated response to bound product concentration and infer residual reactant amounts from the1:1 balance. Explain uncertainties, the model assumptions and why macroscopic stability alone does not measure equal nonzero microscopic rates. Collect waste and close the lab record."
        }
      ],
      "samplingAndEquilibration": "The original S is measured directly in the same capped undiluted cuvette at controlled temperature; the optical measurement adds no reagents. No quench is claimed. If a cuvette/aliquot is diluted, heated, complexed or measured after a temperature change, allow/document the new equilibrium and treat it as a different chemical state. Keeping an aliquot bottle or measuring later is not proof of preservation.",
      "calibrationValidity": "Correct Fe-background separately because standards and equilibrium solutions contain different Fe totals. Calibrant absorbance/initialSCN slope s equals εlα for a single complex,0<α≤1; complete conversion is not proved by a straight line. With transferable εl and the1:1 model, x=Aeq/(εl)≤Aeq/s. This upper bound is sufficient for positive residual-reactant amounts and avoids falsely declaring absolute product concentration/quantitative K from assumed100% standard conversion. If spectral/speciation or matrix transfer fails, even this bound is unavailable.",
      "sourceOperatorsAssessed": "Named chemical coexistence demonstration, suitable preparations/controls, original-state observation and justified species conclusion. Ester equilibrium concentration measurement, model-exchange execution, reaction mechanism, Nernst and other sibling operators are not silently fulfilled."
